---
/* An addendum, not part of the ladder. Someone who passed a British test years ago
   arrives holding a name that no longer exists anywhere on the site, and the only
   published conversion — field moves to Skills — is a table in BIS's own handbook.
   Nothing here links through to an element: the older schemes' requirements are not
   recorded in the guide, so this is a lookup for a name and nothing more. */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Unverified from '../../components/Unverified.astro';

/* From the transition table in the BIS National Skills Tests handbook. Read as data
   rather than transcribed as a table: the columns are ours and the numbers are theirs. */
const transition = [
  { old: '1',  interim: '1',       skills: '1', next: '2'   },
  { old: '2',  interim: '2',       skills: '2', next: '3'   },
  { old: '3',  interim: '3',       skills: '3', next: '4'   },
  { old: '4',  interim: 'BN',      skills: '5', next: '6'   },
  { old: '5',  interim: 'BN',      skills: '5', next: '6'   },
  { old: '6',  interim: 'INT',     skills: '6', next: '7'   },
  { old: '7',  interim: 'INT',     skills: '6', next: '7'   },
  { old: '8',  interim: 'Adv Nov', skills: '7', next: '8'   },
  { old: '9',  interim: 'JNR',     skills: '8', next: null  },
  { old: '10', interim: 'SNR',     skills: '8', next: null  },
];
---
<Base title="Older test names" subtitle="What the older British test levels were called, and what they convert to.">
  <h1>Older test names</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    You passed something years ago and the name has since gone. This page holds the older
    British test names and, where British Ice Skating has published a conversion, what the
    modern equivalent is.
  </p>

  <Unverified verified={{ checked: false }} what="names and dates" />

  <h2>Four sets of names</h2>

  <p>
    The body renamed itself as well as its tests. The National Ice Skating Association began
    rebranding as British Ice Skating in November 2018, so NISA and BIS are the same
    organisation and an old NISA pass is a British Ice Skating pass.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>The medal tests.</b> Preliminary, Inter-Bronze, Bronze, Inter-Silver, Silver,
    Inter-Gold and Gold, with Gold Star above them for free skating only. A level was
    several separate tests rather than one: moves in the field, elements and free skating
    were taken independently and at different paces. So Inter-Silver names a rung on the
    ladder rather than a single pass, which matters below.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Levels 1 to 10.</b> By July 2015 the singles test manual sets out ten numbered levels
    and makes no mention of the medal names. The field moves test at each of those levels is
    the one the Skills tests replaced, and it is what the handbook means by old field moves.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Interim field moves.</b> A short-lived set that kept 1, 2 and 3 and then switched to
    the competition categories — BN, INT, Adv Nov, JNR, SNR. The handbook does not expand the
    abbreviations. Reading them as basic novice, intermediate novice, advanced novice, junior
    and senior matches the category ladder, but it is a reading and not a quotation.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Skills 1 to 8.</b> The current tests, and the ones this guide covers. British Ice
    Skating revised them on 01/10/2026, adding an exercise or two to most levels rather than
    replacing anything.
  </p>

  <h2>What converts to what</h2>

  <p>
    One conversion is published, and it is the field moves one. The table in the National
    Skills Tests handbook awards a Skills pass for a field moves pass and names the test to
    take next.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table class="ladder">
      <caption>
        The conversion is British Ice Skating's, from the <i>National Skills Tests</i>{' '}
        handbook — reproduced here as a historical reference for passes that predate Skills.
        The columns and everything written around them are ours. Check it against{' '}
        <a href="https://www.iceskating.org.uk/test-information">BIS's own test information</a>{' '}
        before you rely on it.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Old field moves</th>
          <th>Interim field moves</th>
          <th>Skills awarded</th>
          <th>Next Skills test</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {transition.map(r => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.old}</td>
            <td>{r.interim}</td>
            <td>{r.skills}</td>
            <td>{r.next ?? '—'}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Ten rungs become eight, so the mapping is not one to one and the compression is all at
    the top. Levels 4 and 5 both award Skills 5, 6 and 7 both award Skills 6, and 9 and 10
    both award Skills 8 with nothing left to take. Nobody arrives at Skills 4 by
    conversion at all: it is a test you take rather than one you are given.
  </p>

  <h2>The medal tests do not convert</h2>

  <p>
    There is no published mapping from Inter-Bronze or Inter-Silver onto a numbered level,
    and none onto Skills. Two renamings sit in between, and a medal level was a set of tests
    rather than a single pass, so there is no one number for it to become. Anyone who offers
    you an exact equivalence is guessing, and this guide will not add to the guessing.
  </p>

  <p>
    The answerable version of the question is what you can hold now. Skills asks for specific
    exercises at each level, so the way in is to read the level pages and find where you
    stop. What credit an older pass carries is a question for your coach and the BIS office,
    and the table above is the whole of what BIS publishes about it.
  </p>

  <p>
    One trap worth naming: bronze, silver and gold appear twice in British skating. The Skate
    UK awards use the same three words at a far earlier stage, so a Skate UK gold badge and an
    old Gold medal test are not the same thing, and are nowhere near the same standard.
  </p>

  <h2>Ice dance changed separately</h2>

  <p>
    Dance moved to a new test structure on 01/09/2025 with its own chart for carrying existing
    awards across and a transition period running to April 2026. That chart is published as an
    image in BIS's announcement, so it is not reproduced or paraphrased here — read it there.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this comes from</h2>

  <ul class="sources">
    <li>The numbered levels: the NISA singles test manual of July 2015.</li>
    <li>
      The medal test names: legacy NISA material still circulating on club sites. No current
      BIS document lists them, which is the main reason this page is marked unverified.
    </li>
    <li>
      Ice dance: British Ice Skating's{' '}
      <a href="https://www.iceskating.org.uk/post/new-ice-dance-test-structure">new ice dance test structure</a>{' '}
      announcement.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>

<style>
  .scroll-x{overflow-x:auto;margin:.25rem 0 1.25rem}
  table.ladder{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;min-width:22rem;
    font-family:ui-sans-serif,-apple-system,sans-serif;font-size:.88rem}
  /* The attribution belongs to the table rather than to the page, so it is the
     table's caption and sits under it. Two copies of it would be one too many. */
  table.ladder caption{caption-side:bottom;text-align:left;padding:.7rem .6rem 0;
    font-size:.82rem;color:var(--ink-soft);line-height:1.5}
  table.ladder th{text-align:left;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:.7rem;
    letter-spacing:.07em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:.5rem .6rem .45rem;
    border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}
  table.ladder td{padding:.55rem .6rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);
    font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
  table.ladder td:nth-child(3){font-weight:600}
  table.ladder td:last-child{color:var(--ink-soft)}
  ul.sources{padding-left:1.1rem;font-size:.9rem;color:var(--ink-soft)}
  ul.sources li{margin:.4rem 0}
</style>
